<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keladi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Keladi is a village in the Shimoga district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a> hills near Sagar. It was the original seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi-nayakas">Keladi Nayakas</a>, local chiefs who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> from about 1499 and later ruled the region independently from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Bidanur</a>. Its Rameshwara temple dates from their rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keladi.jpg" alt="Keladi" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dineshkannambadi 16:39, 26 May 2008 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rameshwara_Temple_in_Keladi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Keladi is a temple town in Sagara Taluk, about 8 km from the town of Sagara. Its principal building is the double temple of Rameshwara and Virabhadra, a large and plain structure in the Hoysala-Dravida style. Two mounds called Kalte at the entrance to the town are pointed out as the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifices</a> told in the story of its founding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Keladi is the place from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a> chiefs derived their origin, and tradition tells it thus. Two brothers, Chavuda Gowda and Bhadra Gowda, lived in the village of Hale-bayal with two servants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> and Murari, who worked their fields. When a cow was seen shedding her milk over an ant-hill, Chavuda Gowda dug into it, found a linga and built a small temple over it. The servants later ploughed up an old sword, which leapt out as a serpent to kill any crow that perched on the shed; Chavuda Gowda kept it and named it Nagaramuri.</p><p>The plough then struck a cauldron of treasure. In a dream Chavuda Gowda was told to offer a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> and take it, and the two servants volunteered on condition that their memory be preserved; they were beheaded with Nagaramuri. With this wealth the brothers raised a small force and subdued the neighbouring villages, but they were seized on the king's order and held in custody at Vijayanagar.</p><p>Through the court musicians they offered to punish a rebellious Palegar near Balihalu. Having killed him, they were released, confirmed in the places they had taken and given a royal seal, and the town of Keladi was founded with the temple of Rameshwara. Later, while the chief was hunting, a hare turned on his hounds, a sign of heroic virtue in the soil, and he moved his town to that spot, calling it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a>.</p><p>His son, with the sanction of the Vijayanagar sovereign Sadasiva Raya, took the name Sadasiva-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Battle of Talikota</a> broke up the Vijayanagar Empire, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi-nayakas">Keladi Nayakas</a> created an independent kingdom, whose rulers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivappa-nayaka">Shivappa Nayaka</a>, Chennamma and Basava Raja. It remained independent until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> annexed it to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Keladi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mysore's early armies were raised from local chiefs and peasant infantry, and the Wodeyars fought frequent wars with neighbouring chiefs, the Nayakas of Madurai and <b>Keladi</b>, and the Bijapur Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Keladi Nayakas began as local chiefs at <b>Keladi</b> in the Malnad hills, serving the Vijayanagara Empire as governors from around 1499.</blockquote><a href="/state/keladi-nayaka-kingdom">Keladi Nayaka kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1600 a member of the Haleri family, a Lingayat house related to the Nayakas of Ikkeri (<b>Keladi</b>), established himself in Kodagu, the hilly country at the source of the Kaveri.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-coorg">Kingdom of Coorg</a></li></ul></section>
